{0 Getting Started with OCaml Telegram Bot API}

{1 Installation and Setup}

This guide will walk you through installing [ocaml_telegram_eio] and building your first Telegram bot. You'll learn how to set up your development environment, create a simple bot, and understand the core concepts of the library's elegant functional API.

{2 Prerequisites}

Before you begin, ensure you have:

- {b OCaml 5.0+}: The library requires OCaml 5 for effect handlers and Eio support
- {b opam 2.0+}: OCaml package manager
- {b A Telegram Bot Token}: Obtain from {{:https://t.me/BotFather}BotFather}

{2 Installing OCaml 5}

If you don't have OCaml 5 installed:

{[
# Install OCaml 5.2 (or later)
opam switch create 5.2.0
eval $(opam env)

# Verify version
ocaml --version  # Should show 5.2.0 or higher
]}

{2 Installing the Library}

Install [ocaml_telegram_eio] via opam:

{[
opam install ocaml_telegram_eio
]}

This will install the library and all its dependencies, including:
- [eio] - Effect-based direct-style IO
- [cohttp-eio] - HTTP client built on Eio
- [yojson] - JSON parsing and generation
- [tls-eio] - TLS support for HTTPS

{2 Creating Your Bot Token}

{3 Step 1: Talk to BotFather}

Open Telegram and search for {{:https://t.me/BotFather}BotFather}, then:

{v
1. Send /newbot
2. Choose a display name (e.g., "My First Bot")
3. Choose a username ending in "bot" (e.g., "my_first_ocaml_bot")
4. BotFather will reply with your bot token
v}

Your token looks like: [123456789:ABCdefGHIjklMNOpqrsTUVwxyz]

{3 Step 2: Store Your Token Securely}

{b Never commit tokens to version control!} Store it as an environment variable:

{[
# Add to your ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc
export TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN="your-token-here"

# Or create a .env file (add to .gitignore!)
echo "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=your-token-here" > .env

# Load it in your shell
export $(cat .env | xargs)
]}

{2 Project Setup}

Create a new OCaml project:

{[
# Create project directory
mkdir my_telegram_bot
cd my_telegram_bot

# Initialize dune project
cat > dune-project << 'EOF'
(lang dune 3.0)
(name my_telegram_bot)
EOF

# Create executable configuration
mkdir bin
cat > bin/dune << 'EOF'
(executable
 (name main)
 (libraries eio_main ocaml_telegram_eio.telegram ocaml_telegram_eio.tg))
EOF
]}

{1 Your First Bot: Hello World}

Let's build a simple bot that responds to the [/start] command.

{2 The Complete Code}

Create [bin/main.ml]:

{[
open Eio.Std
open Telegram  (* Core types and API *)
open Tg        (* High-level bot DSL *)

let () =
  (* Start the Eio event loop *)
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->

  (* Get bot token from environment *)
  let token =
    try Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN"
    with Not_found ->
      failwith "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN environment variable not set"
  in

  (* Create HTTP client for Telegram API *)
  let client = Client.create ~env ~token in

  (* Define how to handle incoming updates *)
  let handle_update update =
    (* Pattern match on update type *)
    match update with
    | { Bot.Update.message = Some msg; _ } ->
        (* Extract chat ID and message text *)
        let chat_id = msg.Message.chat.Chat.id in
        let text = Option.value msg.Message.text ~default:"" in

        (* Respond to /start command *)
        if text = "/start" then
          let reply = "👋 Hello! I'm your first OCaml Telegram bot!" in
          let request = Api.send_message ~chat_id ~text:reply () in

          (* Make API call *)
          let* _response = Api.call client request in
          Ok ()
        else
          Ok ()  (* Ignore other messages *)

    | _ -> Ok ()  (* Ignore non-message updates *)
  in

  (* Start long polling (blocking call) *)
  traceln "🤖 Bot started! Send /start to interact...";
  Polling.run client handle_update
]}

{2 Understanding the Code}

Let's break down the key concepts:

{3 Eio Event Loop}

{[
Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
]}

- [Eio_main.run] starts the Eio runtime
- [env] provides system resources (network, filesystem, etc.)
- All your async code runs within this context

{3 Client Creation}

{[
let client = Client.create ~env ~token in
]}

- Creates an HTTP client for Telegram API
- [~env] provides network access from Eio
- [~token] authenticates your bot

{3 Update Handler}

{[
let handle_update update =
  match update with
  | { Bot.Update.message = Some msg; _ } -> ...
]}

- Pattern matching on update structure
- Updates can be messages, callbacks, inline queries, etc.
- We extract the message if present

{3 API Calls}

{[
let request = Api.send_message ~chat_id ~text:reply () in
let* _response = Api.call client request in
]}

- [Api.send_message] creates a type-safe request builder
- [Api.call] executes the request with automatic error handling
- [let*] is Result syntax for handling errors elegantly

{3 Long Polling}

{[
Polling.run client handle_update
]}

- Continuously polls Telegram for new updates
- Handles offset management automatically
- Calls [handle_update] for each update

{2 Running Your Bot}

Build and run:

{[
# Build the project
dune build

# Run your bot
dune exec bin/main.exe
]}

You should see:
{v
🤖 Bot started! Send /start to interact...
v}

Now open Telegram, find your bot, and send [/start]. The bot will reply with a greeting!

{2 Adding More Features}

Let's enhance the bot to echo all messages:

{[
let handle_update update =
  match update with
  | { Bot.Update.message = Some msg; _ } ->
      let chat_id = msg.Message.chat.Chat.id in
      let text = Option.value msg.Message.text ~default:"" in

      (* Handle different commands *)
      let reply = match text with
        | "/start" -> "👋 Hello! Send me any message and I'll echo it back."
        | "/help" -> "Just send me text and I'll echo it!"
        | _ -> Printf.sprintf "You said: %s" text
      in

      let request = Api.send_message ~chat_id ~text:reply () in
      let* _response = Api.call client request in
      Ok ()

  | _ -> Ok ()
]}

{1 Core Concepts}

{2 The Bot Lifecycle}

Every bot follows this pattern:

{v
1. Initialize Eio runtime
2. Create HTTP client with token
3. Define update handler(s)
4. Start polling or webhook server
5. Handle incoming updates
6. Make API calls to respond
v}

{2 Update Processing Model}

Updates are the core data structure:

{[
type update = {
  update_id : int;                    (* Unique update ID *)
  message : Message.t option;         (* New incoming message *)
  edited_message : Message.t option;  (* Edited message *)
  callback_query : CallbackQuery.t option;  (* Button click *)
  (* ... and many more update types *)
}
]}

Pattern match to handle different update types:

{[
match update with
| { message = Some msg; _ } -> handle_message msg
| { callback_query = Some cb; _ } -> handle_callback cb
| { inline_query = Some iq; _ } -> handle_inline iq
| _ -> Ok ()  (* Ignore unknown updates *)
]}

{2 Error Handling Philosophy}

The library uses [Result.t] for error handling:

{[
type ('a, 'e) result = Ok of 'a | Error of 'e

(* API calls return Result *)
let* response = Api.call client request in
(* response : (Message.t, Error.t) result *)
]}

Use [let*] syntax for elegant error propagation:

{[
let handle_update update =
  match update with
  | { message = Some msg; _ } ->
      (* If any step fails, the whole function returns Error *)
      let* user_info = Api.call client (Api.get_me ()) in
      let* sent_msg = Api.call client (Api.send_message ...) in
      let* edited = Api.call client (Api.edit_message ...) in
      Ok ()
  | _ -> Ok ()
]}

Errors are categorized as retryable or permanent:

{[
match Api.call client request with
| Ok result -> (* Success! *)
| Error err when Error.is_retryable err ->
    (* Network issue, rate limit - safe to retry *)
| Error err ->
    (* Invalid parameters, forbidden - don't retry *)
]}

{2 Eio Concurrency Primitives}

Handle multiple updates concurrently using Eio fibers:

{[
Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
  (* Process updates concurrently *)
  List.iter (fun update ->
    Eio.Fiber.fork ~sw (fun () ->
      handle_update update
    )
  ) updates

  (* Switch waits for all fibers to complete *)
]}

All fibers in a switch are cleaned up automatically, even on errors.

{1 Environment Variables}

{2 Required Variables}

- [TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN]: Your bot authentication token

{2 Optional Variables}

- [TELEGRAM_API_BASE]: Override API base URL (default: [https://api.telegram.org])
- [RUST_LOG] or [OCAML_LOG]: Enable debug logging

{2 Loading Environment Variables}

{3 From Shell}

{[
export TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN="your-token"
dune exec bin/main.exe
]}

{3 From .env File}

{[
# Install dotenv support
opam install dotenv

(* In your code *)
Dotenv.export () ;; (* Load .env file *)
let token = Sys.getenv "TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN"
]}

{3 From Config File}

{[
(* config.ml *)
type config = {
  token : string;
  api_base : string;
}

let load_config () =
  (* Read from JSON, TOML, etc. *)
  Yojson.Safe.from_file "config.json"
  |> Yojson.Safe.Util.to_assoc
  |> ...
]}

{1 Development Workflow}

{2 Project Structure}

Recommended file organization:

{v
my_telegram_bot/
├── bin/
│   ├── dune
│   └── main.ml          # Entry point
├── lib/
│   ├── dune
│   ├── handlers.ml      # Update handlers
│   ├── commands.ml      # Command definitions
│   └── keyboards.ml     # Keyboard builders
├── test/
│   ├── dune
│   └── test_handlers.ml # Unit tests
├── dune-project
├── .env                 # Secrets (gitignored!)
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
v}

{2 Running Locally}

{[
# Build and run
dune exec bin/main.exe

# Watch mode (rebuild on changes)
dune build --watch

# Run with verbose output
RUST_LOG=debug dune exec bin/main.exe
]}

{2 Debugging Techniques}

{3 Enable Debug Logging}

{[
(* Add to your main.ml *)
let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Eio.traceln "Starting bot with token: %s"
    (String.sub token 0 10 ^ "...");

  let handle_update update =
    Eio.traceln "Received update: %d" update.Bot.Update.update_id;
    (* ... *)
  in
  (* ... *)
]}

{3 Print API Responses}

{[
let* response = Api.call client request in
Eio.traceln "Response: %s"
  (Yojson.Safe.to_string (Response.to_yojson response));
Ok response
]}

{3 Handle Errors Explicitly}

{[
match Api.call client request with
| Ok result ->
    Eio.traceln "✓ Success";
    Ok result
| Error err ->
    Eio.traceln "✗ Error: %s" (Error.to_string err);
    Error err
]}

{2 Testing Strategies}

{3 Unit Testing Handlers}

{[
(* test/test_handlers.ml *)
open Telegram

let test_start_command () =
  let msg = {
    Message.message_id = 1;
    chat = { Chat.id = 12345L; type_ = "private"; (* ... *) };
    text = Some "/start";
    (* ... *)
  } in

  let update = { Bot.Update.update_id = 1; message = Some msg; (* ... *) } in

  match Handlers.handle_update update with
  | Ok () -> Printf.printf "✓ /start handled correctly\n"
  | Error _ -> failwith "Handler should succeed"
]}

{3 Integration Testing}

Create a test bot and verify real API calls:

{[
(* Use a separate test bot token *)
let test_bot_token = Sys.getenv "TEST_BOT_TOKEN"

let test_send_message () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  let client = Client.create ~env ~token:test_bot_token in
  let chat_id = Int64.of_string (Sys.getenv "TEST_CHAT_ID") in

  let request = Api.send_message ~chat_id ~text:"Test message" () in
  match Api.call client request with
  | Ok msg ->
      assert (msg.Message.text = Some "Test message");
      Printf.printf "✓ Integration test passed\n"
  | Error err ->
      failwith (Error.to_string err)
]}

